ivided by the coefficient without the root.", "ask": "What power does x carry in your expression?"}, "COEFFICIENT_FORGOTTEN": {"note": "Their x is the root of Ksp alone: the 4 or the 27 was left out.", "ask": "What multiplies x to the power in your expression?"}, "S_FOR_ION": {"note": "They gave x where the ion's concentration was asked, though two ions form per formula unit.", "ask": "How many of that ion does each dissolved formula unit put into the water?"}, "GRAMS_WITHOUT_MOLAR_MASS": {"note": "Their grams per litre equal the molar solubility: the molar mass was not used.", "ask": "What converts moles per litre into grams per litre?"}, "COMMON_ION_IGNORED": {"note": "Their x is the pure-water solubility: the ion already present was not counted.", "ask": "What concentration of that ion was in the water before any solid dissolved?"}, "SMALL_S_WHEN_IT_FAILS": {"note": "They dropped x next to the common ion, but x is not small next to it here.", "ask": "Compare your x with the concentration already present. Is it small next to it?"}, "VOLUME_NOT_TOTALED": {"note": "A concentration divided by the other solution's volume, not the total.", "ask": "Which volume holds the ions after mixing?"}, "DILUTION_IGNORED": {"note": "A concentration before mixing used as if it were after mixing.", "ask": "Mixing spreads each solute through both volumes. What is the total volume?"}, "Q_INVERTED": {"note": "Their Q is upside down.", "ask": "Q has the same form as Ksp. Which concentrations go into the product?"}, "EXPONENTS_DROPPED": {"note": "Ksp (or Q) from the ion concentrations with every exponent left out.", "ask": "Is each concentration raised to its count in the formula?"}, "COEFFICIENTS_AS_MULTIPLIERS": {"note": "A coefficient multiplied in where it should be an exponent.", "ask": "Is the 2 a multiplier on the concentration, or the power it is raised to?"}, "PRECIPITATE_WHEN_Q_LESS": {"note": "They predicted a precipitate with Q below Ksp.", "ask": "Is Q above or below Ksp? Which way must the ions go?"}, "NO_PRECIPITATE_WHEN_Q_MORE": {"note": "They predicted no precipitate with Q above Ksp.", "ask": "Q is above Ksp. Can the solution hold that many ions?"}, "SOLUBILITY_RISES_WITH_COMMON_ION": {"note": "They predicted more dissolving when a common ion was added.", "ask": "The added ion sits on which side of the dissolving equation, and which way does that push it?"}}, "trap_notes_by_opener": {}, "equation_symbols": {"Q": ["Qsp", "Q_sp", "Q"], "K": ["Ksp", "K_sp", "K"]}, "equation_constants": {}, "openers": [{"title": "The Chloride Test", "question": "Water is tested for chloride by adding silver nitrate: a white cloud means chloride. The stockroom worker adds 1.0 mL of 0.010 M silver nitrate to 9.0 mL of tap water, and a turbidity probe jumps as a cloud forms. The same drop into deionized water leaves the probe at zero. Why does a precipitate form in one sample and not the other?", "model_answer": "After mixing (10.0 mL total): [Ag⁺] = 1.0 × 10⁻³ M; tap water [Cl⁻] = 9.0 × 10⁻⁴ M. Qsp = [Ag⁺][Cl⁻] = 9.0 × 10⁻⁷ > Ksp 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁰ (Appendix J): AgCl precipitates until Q = Ksp. Deionized water (Cl⁻ ≈ 9 × 10⁻⁹ M after mixing): Q ≈ 9 × 10⁻¹² < Ksp: no precipitate. When Q > Ksp, Ag⁺–Cl⁻ collisions build solid faster than it dissolves.", "target": "A precipitate forms when Q, from the concentrations after mixing, exceeds Ksp; below Ksp the ions stay dissolved.", "keywords": ["Q", "Ksp", "compare", "after mixing", "silver ion", "chloride", "collide", "precipitate", "exceeds", "cloud"], "liquids": ["CT1", "CT2", "CT3", "CT4", "CT5", "CT6"], "kinds": ["precipitates", "stays_dissolved"], "fits": ["CT1", "CT2", "CT3", "CT4", "CT5", "CT6"], "media": {"image_with_question": "ch15/Chloride_test.jpg", "image_after_account": "", "image_alt": "A stockroom bench: a cloudy tube labeled tap water and a clear tube labeled deionized, a dropper bottle labeled 0.010 M AgNO3, and a turbidity meter reading 85 NTU.", "simulation": "ch15/solubility_simulator.html?mode=mix&salt={salt}&c_cat={c_cat}&c_an={c_an}&theme={theme}", "simulation_opens": "meaning", "credit": "AI image made by the instructor with Gemini"}, "label_words": ["precipitation", "Qsp", "Ksp", "supersaturated", "reaction quotient"], "role": "worked", "items": {"add": [], "remove": []}}, {"title": "The Kidney Stone Risk", "question": "A kidney-stone lab mixes calcium solutions with oxalate solutions, the two ions in most stones, and watches a turbidity probe for the first cloud of calcium oxalate. Some mixtures cloud and some stay clear. What decides whether a mixture clouds, and which ions collide to build the solid?", "model_answer": "Ca²⁺ and C₂O₄²⁻ collide to build CaC₂O₄. After mixing, each concentration = c × V(own) ÷ V(total); Qsp = [Ca²⁺][C₂O₄²⁻]. If Qsp > Ksp (1.96 × 10⁻⁹, Appendix J), solid forms until Q = Ksp; if Qsp < Ksp, the mixture stays clear.", "target": "Whether a precipitate forms depends on Q after mixing compared with Ksp.", "keywords": ["calcium ion", "oxalate", "collide", "Q", "Ksp", "compare", "after mixing", "total volume", "precipitate", "exceeds"], "liquids": ["KR1", "KR2", "KR3", "KR4", "KR5", "KR6"], "kinds": ["precipitates", "stays_dissolved"], "fits": ["KR1", "KR2", "KR3", "KR4", "KR5", "KR6"], "media": {"image_with_question": "ch15/Kidney_stone_risk.jpg", "image_after_account": "", "image_alt": "A lab bench: a rack of tubes, some cloudy and some clear, bottles labeled calcium chloride and sodium oxalate, and a turbidity meter reading 12 NTU.", "simulation": "ch15/solubility_simulator.html?mode=mix&salt={salt}&c_cat={c_cat}&c_an={c_an}&theme={theme}", "simulation_opens": "meaning", "credit": "AI image made by the instructor with Gemini"}, "label_words": ["precipitation", "Qsp", "Ksp", "kidney stone", "reaction quotient"], "role": "exam", "items": {"add": [], "remove": []}}, {"title": "The Golden Rain", "question": "Two clear, colorless solutions, lead(II) nitrate and potassium iodide, are poured together, and a brilliant yellow solid, 